   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e361046a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The lump-sum tax lies weakly above every product tax in both calibrations, so it is the cheapest way to raise any given revenue: it lowers income without touching a single relative price, so there is no substitution effect and no excess burden. The tax on all three goods lies exactly on top of it, and that is no accident — a uniform tax leaves relative prices unchanged, so the shares do not move, revenue is $R = \\frac{\\tau}{1+\\tau}I$ and real income is $\\frac{I}{1+\\tau} = I-R$. It is a lump-sum tax in disguise. The rest depends on the calibration: under complements the four narrow taxes all hug the lump-sum line, since the consumer cannot escape any of them, while under substitutes the train tax is clearly worst and its curve even bends backwards — past $\\tau\\approx0.59$ the government raises less revenue and the consumer is worse off, so that whole stretch is dominated."
   ]
