    "**Ranking.** The order is the same in both calibrations: lump-sum = tax on all three goods, then food, then bus and train together, and last one of the two narrow travel taxes. What drives it is simply how easily the consumer can get away from the taxed good, and for the first instruments the excess burden is below 1 percent of the revenue. The train tax is the interesting case: 8.1 percent and a 1.6 percent loss under complements, but 23.6 percent and a 28.5 percent loss under substitutes, where the consumer switches to the bus and the tax base shrinks — and where $R=0.20$ is already 77 percent of the most this tax can ever raise. This is the Ramsey lesson: a narrow tax on a good with a close substitute is the most expensive way to raise revenue, and the cost depends on the substitution elasticity, not on the rate."
