    "Two things stand out. First, the same 20 percent cut costs about 0.15 units of income under complements but only 0.07 under substitutes, because that consumer can keep travelling and simply switch from bus to train while the other has to travel less — so the elasticity of substitution inside the nest, which appears nowhere in the emission accounting, is what decides how expensive climate policy is. Second, the blunt instrument is much worse: the uniform travel tax raises the price of the clean mode as much as the dirty one, so it can only cut emissions by cutting travel, and it costs 0.22 in both calibrations — three times the targeted tax under substitutes. The emission-proportional tax is cheapest in both, but only barely better than taxing the bus alone, which makes sense since the bus does most of the polluting anyway."
