   "source": [
    "**Conclusion.**\n",
    "\n",
    "Both calibrations end up at almost the same tax: about 31 percent on the bus and 4 percent on the train. The ratio is 7.5 and 7.8, which is what the Pigouvian rule $\\tau_j = \\delta e_j/(\\lambda p_j)$ says it should be — $\\lambda$ is the same for both goods and cancels, leaving $(e_2/p_2)/(e_3/p_3) = 7.5$. The optimizer never saw that formula, so it is a real check on the solution. The levels come out a little above the naive benchmark because travel gets dearer and $\\lambda$ falls.\n",
    "\n",
    "What changes across calibrations is not the tax but what it achieves. The same rates cut emissions by 10 percent under complements and by 25 percent under substitutes, and the welfare gain is nearly four times larger in the latter (0.044 against 0.012). So the right green tax depends only on damages and prices, while its effect depends on how easily people can switch to the clean option — an argument for making the train a close substitute for the bus before taxing the bus, or at least at the same time.\n",
    "\n",
    "The welfare surface also shows why this is numerically awkward. It is very flat near the optimum, especially in the $\\tau_3$ direction: the grid search and L-BFGS-B agree on welfare to four decimals but still differ in the second decimal of the tax rates. That is the same flat-surface problem as in question 2.1.4, and the reason the tolerances in 2.2.4 matter."
   ]
